WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.300
For every 100 acres idled by the Conservation Reserve Program, farmers converted 20 acres of

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:14.040
other land to crops. This is unintended consequences, episode 140. In the Central United States, late

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:19.960
in 1980s, a federal contract paid a farmer to stop planting one field. The soil on that

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:26.200
field was supposed to rest. Erosion was supposed to slow. On a neighboring parcel that same farmer

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:31.580
still owned. New ground was cleared and planted instead. The land taken out of production

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:37.300
was measured and celebrated. The land brought into production was not. That gap had a name

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:43.280
among the economists who later measured it. Slipage. It did not begin as a trick. It began as

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:49.260
a contract that looked on the drafting table almost too clean to fail. The Conservation Reserve

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:54.120
serve program, CRP, was written into the 1985 Food Security Act.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:59.020
The years before that bill had been full of visible soil loss across the Great Plains

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:00.000
and the Midwest.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.200
Sheet erosion cut fields.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.500
Wind lifted topsoil off summer-fallowed ground and carried it for miles.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:12.500
Lawmakers and officials at the United States Department of Agriculture, the USDA, wanted

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:13.580
three things at once.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.960
They wanted less water erosion and less wind erosion.

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.700
They wanted smaller federal grain surpluses, and they wanted farmers to have a reliable rental

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:27.100
payment if those farmers would retire highly erodable acres for 10-year periods.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.780
At the time, the policy looked straightforward.

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:35.580
Pay landowners to take marginal crop land out of annual tillage, and the environmental gains

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:36.640
would follow directly.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:42.380
June J. Wu later documented how widely that expectation was shared among the people who designed

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:42.980
the program.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:48.960
them. The contracts were voluntary. The payments were set by competitive bids. Early enrollment

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.860
targets were met quickly, because many operators already knew which fields were losing top

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:59.260
soil fastest. No one at the drafting table treated every other acre on a farm as a fixed

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:04.820
quantity. The assumption was that retired acres would stay retired. The arithmetic seemed

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:10.460
simple enough on paper. If an operator idled 100 acres that had been losing 8 tons of soil

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:15.260
per acre each year to sheet erosion, the gross saving would be 800 tons a year.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:21.320
Picture that, 800 tons of soil that would not leave those 100 acres, the same kind of

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.200
calculation applied to wind erosion on the northern plains.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:29.400
There, blowing dust from summer-fallowed fields could remove several inches of topsoil

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:30.660
in a single dry season.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.480
Several inches, not a film of dust, a depth you could measure with a ruler after one bad

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:36.840
year.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:42.140
Because the program used a bid system, the agency could select the most erosive parcels

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:42.560
first.

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.980
The rental rate was calibrated to the local cash rent market, so participation would

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:49.820
be attractive without overpaying.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:55.100
Designers therefore expected the measured reductions on contracted acres to translate almost

00:02:55.100 --> 00:03:00.860
one-for-one into watershed-level improvements, less sediment in the water, better downstream

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:01.760
water quality.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:04.600
The logic was not careless, it was linear.

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:10.220
year. Retire the worst field, count the soil that field no longer loses, treat that

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:15.000
count as the gain for the watershed. What the contract did not do was ask what the

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:21.100
rental check might finance on land the contract never touched. By 1990, more than 30 million

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:27.120
acres had been accepted into CRP contracts across the central United States. 30 million acres

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.980
is a landscape, not a pilot plot. The Department of Agriculture published annual reports showing

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:38.520
large measured drops in sheet and rail erosion on the enrolled parcels. Proponents pointed

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:43.300
to those numbers as proof the rental payments were working, and on the enrolled parcels,

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:48.020
the numbers were real. Soil that had been leaving those fields was leaving more slowly,

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:54.020
or not at all, while the contracts held. At the same time, a few agricultural economists began

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:58.960
asking a different question. Were the acres that remained in production changing their own use?

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.240
Shinsuke Uchido would later use farm-level census records to test exactly that.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:07.760
The early success stories focused on the contracted fields.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.740
The data that could reveal substitution across other land on the same farms were not yet

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:13.160
assembled.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:18.780
Early implementation reports therefore recorded only the acres inside contract boundaries.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:24.960
When a county reported 50,000 acres enrolled, the erosion reduction tables subtracted the

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.600
historic erosion rates for those 50,000 acres and stopped there.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:33.940
The possibility that the same operator might now plant an additional 10,000 acres on

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:37.980
other ground under the same ownership sat outside the accounting frame.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:39.920
That was not a hidden footnote.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.460
It was the frame itself.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:47.400
If the contract boundary was the unit of measurement, anything beyond that boundary did not enter

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:48.240
the subtraction.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:53.100
Satellite imagery from the period later showed something the tables had not been built to

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:53.340
see.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:58.900
Some of the newly cultivated parcels had been hay meadows or woodlots, converting them

00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:02.000
required clearing, drainage tiling, or breaking sod.

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:07.400
Those operations themselves generated short-term erosion spikes before the new crop land

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:13.140
stabilized, so the program could be reducing erosion on a contracted field, while a clearing

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:17.440
crew, paid in part by the rental income, opened a meadow next door.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:21.400
Both things could be true in the same season, on the same ownership.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:25.060
The reports that reached Washington counted only one of them.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:31.320
June J. Wu's 2000 study in the American Journal of Agricultural Economics examined county

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:33.880
level data across the central United States.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:40.620
Wu found that for every 100 acres enrolled in CRP, roughly 20 acres of non-crop land were

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:41.520
converted to crops.

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:47.020
Those conversions landed on land that had previously been pasture, hay, or woodland.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.880
20 acres for every 100 retired.

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:54.760
not a rounding error at the edge of a spreadsheet. A fifth of the retired area

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:59.880
showing up again as new crop land. And those new acres were often more erosive than

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:05.040
the average retired parcel. That detail changes the arithmetic. The program had

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:09.500
been designed to retire the most erosive ground first through competitive bids.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:14.580
The ground coming into crops was not a random swap of like for like. Because the

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:19.300
new acres were often more erosive, the net reduction in water erosion was about

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:25.520
9 percent smaller than the gross figure implied. The net reduction in wind erosion was about 14 percent

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:31.980
smaller. 9 percent and 14 percent are not the whole story disappearing. They are the story shrinking

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:37.240
in a way the annual reports had not been written to show. A watershed that was promised the

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:43.060
full gross saving received something thinner. Shinsuke Uchita's 2011 analysis of agricultural

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:48.420
census longitudinal records reached a similar conclusion at the individual farm level.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:54.640
Partial participants converted an average of 25% of their remaining non-crop land to cropping

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:55.480
after enrollment.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:02.340
Eucita's 2014 working paper refined the estimate to 14%, using fixed-effects methods

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.200
that controlled for time-invariant farm characteristics.

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:13.780
14% in one specification, 25% in another, roughly 20 acres per 100 in Wu's county data.

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:16.520
The studies do not land on a single integer.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:20.200
Here, they land on a range, and the range points the same direction.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:22.620
Enrollment was followed by conversion.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:24.060
The mechanism was simple.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:29.700
A farmer who received CRP rental income on part of the operation could use that income

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:32.740
to cover the costs of clearing or draining another part.

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:36.980
The program rule paid only for the acres inside the contract boundary.

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:40.000
It placed no restriction on the acres outside it.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:45.220
The result was slippage that appeared in satellite imagery as well as in census tabulations.

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:50.740
actions. David Fleming's 2010 study documented that pattern across more than 1,000 northern

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:57.140
plains and corn belt counties. More than 1,000 counties, not one unusual township. The

00:07:57.140 --> 00:08:02.400
second order effect followed from the first. Total cultivated area declined less than program

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:07.460
administrators expected. So some of the sediment and nutrient reductions that had been forecast

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:12.760
never materialized at the watershed scale. The field level celebration and the watershed

00:08:12.760 --> 00:08:14.800
level outcome were not the same number.

00:08:15.460 --> 00:08:20.040
One objection sometimes raised is that the converted acres were marginal and would have

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:21.520
been cropped eventually anyway.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:26.280
That objection deserves a straight look, because timing matters if you are trying to know

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:27.300
what a payment caused.

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:32.460
The longitudinal farm records show the timing of conversion clustered immediately after

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:37.620
CRP enrollment on the same ownership, and the magnitude of conversions scaled with the share

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:40.700
of non-crop land the farm held at the moment of enrollment.

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:46.420
Farms that entered the program with large reserves of convertible pasture or woodland showed

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:49.060
the largest subsequent increases in cropped area.

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:54.440
If conversion had been a slow, independent drift toward more crops, you would not expect

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:59.360
it to bunch right after the contract, or to scale with how much convertible land the farm

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:00.060
already held.

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:02.500
The cash flow effect is straightforward.

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:07.120
The annual CRP rental check arrived regardless of commodity prices.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:12.460
That lowered the risk of investing in new drainage or clearing equipment on the unconstrained

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:12.760
acres.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:17.820
A farmer staring at a volatile grain market might hesitate to tile a wet pasture.

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:23.440
A farmer staring at that same market with a 10-year rental check already in the plan had

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:23.860
a cushion.

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:28.880
The payment did not require the operator to keep non-enrolled land in its pre-enrollment

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:29.300
state.

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:35.160
So the payment effectively subsidized the expansion of the cultivated footprint rather than simply

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:35.800
shrinking it.

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:38.940
That is the causal chain stated without drama.

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:44.660
Retire a field, receive rent, use the rent to open ground the contract never mentioned.

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:48.520
Watch the net erosion gain come in smaller than the gross count.

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:53.520
Then watch the watershed miss part of the sediment reduction that the gross count had been

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:54.280
used to promise.

00:09:55.000 --> 00:09:58.960
Third order, the political success of the program rested on the gross numbers.

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:04.420
Those numbers were easier to publish because they lived inside the contract boundary where

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:05.620
measurement was required.

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:09.460
The slippage lived outside that boundary, where measurement was not.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:15.000
A program can be popular and even genuinely helpful on the acres it pays for, while

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.100
still overstating what the landscape gained.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:22.100
Later CRP rules added sodbuster and swampbuster compliance requirements.

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:25.960
Those rules tied benefits to the treatment of non-enrolled land.

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:28.720
Sodbusters spoke to newly broken sod.

00:10:29.340 --> 00:10:30.880
Swampbusters spoke to wetlands.

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:35.580
plans. They were an attempt to say that a payment on one parcel could not be entirely silent

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:40.780
about what happened on other parcels under the same operation. Yet the core rental contract

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:46.020
still covered only the acres bid into the program. Researchers continued to publish slippage

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:51.660
estimates. Some auction designs began to incorporate points that penalize farms with large reserves

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:57.240
of convertible non-crop land. If a farm held a big stock of pasture or woodland that could

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:01.960
be brought into crops, the scoring could treat that stock as a reason to bid more carefully,

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:04.060
or not to select that bid at all.

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:07.680
No large-scale reversal of existing contracts occurred.

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:11.500
The acres already under 10-year agreements stayed under those agreements.

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:17.260
The program itself was reauthorized in subsequent farm bills with tighter environmental criteria.

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:19.180
The basic tension remained.

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:21.000
Pay for retired acres.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:24.440
Leave other land on the same ownership free to change use.

00:11:24.440 --> 00:11:28.040
Compliance conditions and auction points narrowed that tension.

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:31.500
They did not erase the contract structure that had produced it.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:34.980
It is worth sitting with what the redesign did and did not do.

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:37.840
It did not treat the original designers as fools.

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:42.980
It treated the original measurement frame as incomplete and tried to add rules around

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:44.880
a contract that had already been written.

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:50.020
Adding a compliance condition after enrollment has begun is a different political act from

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:52.000
writing that condition into the first bid.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:56.920
it. Farmers had already planned around the rental check. Counties had already reported

00:11:56.920 --> 00:12:01.880
the gross erosion drops. The later rules had to live beside contracts that still paid

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:07.120
only inside the boundary. That is why the slippage estimates kept coming, even after

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:12.120
sodbuster and swampbuster were on the books. The fixed-addressed treatment of non-enrolled

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:17.960
land. It did not rewrite the unit the program was built to count. When a payment rule rewards

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:23.000
the retirement of one parcel, and leaves every other parcel on the same ownership free to change

00:12:23.000 --> 00:12:28.120
use. The measured gain on the paid parcel will overstate the net change across the whole farm.

00:12:28.820 --> 00:12:33.700
Wu's county figures and Uchita's farm records are what that overstatement looked like in numbers.

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:41.460
A gross saving, then a net saving, 9-14% smaller, with 14-25 acres of new crops appearing against

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:47.760
every 100 retired. A second principle sits inside the same records. Slipage grew larger when

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:53.200
participants held more convertible non-crop land at the moment they enrolled because that capacity

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:58.060
is what the payment freed them to exercise. The check did not create pasture out of nothing.

00:12:58.580 --> 00:13:03.920
It lowered the risk of converting pasture the farm already had. A third principle is about sequence.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:09.820
Adding compliance conditions on non-enrolled land after enrollment began was an attempt to close a

00:13:09.820 --> 00:13:16.340
gap the original contracts had left open. The attempt was real, the gap was structural. Closing at

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:21.260
Halfway, after the checks were already flowing, is not the same as deciding, at the bid,

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:25.020
which acres outside the boundary a program is willing to watch.

00:13:25.540 --> 00:13:28.800
Environmental Intelligence covers the regulatory side daily.

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:34.680
How might a new land retirement program today decide which acres outside the contract boundary

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:36.540
is willing to monitor and constrain?

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:39.140
And that's where today's story leaves us.

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:43.320
If it changed how you see one everyday decision, that's the whole point.

